WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:01.560
- [Justin Lee] My biggest
teaching challenge

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:03.704
is the oversized classroom, because I want

00:00:03.704 --> 00:00:06.990
to create genuine
relationships with my students.

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:09.210
I love teaching, especially music,

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:11.910
because I get to share my
passion with my students,

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:14.040
in the hopes it'll
inspire them to find what

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.663
they're passionate about
and to follow their dreams.

00:00:16.663 --> 00:00:18.327
(audience applauds)

00:00:18.327 --> 00:00:20.007
- Welcome back, that was Justin Lee,

00:00:20.007 --> 00:00:22.950
an orchestra and band teacher
at Mark Keppel High school

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:24.360
in Alhambra, California.

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:26.130
He's one of dozens of teachers

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:28.920
in our audience, joining
us on World Teachers Day.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:32.430
He's with us, along with the
First Lady, Dr. Jill Biden.

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:33.903
Give it up!

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:39.600
So, by a show of hands,
who believes teaching

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.240
has never been more
challenging than right now.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:43.739
Yeah. Oh my god, we got a wave.

00:00:43.739 --> 00:00:44.880
(laughs)

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.910
She was like, "Help!"
(laughs)

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:48.810
That was awesome.

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:51.420
It was more than just a
hand raise. I see you.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:54.630
So, last month I visited Uvalde, Texas.

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:57.540
I'm a Texas girl, and 19
students and 2 teachers

00:00:57.540 --> 00:00:59.700
were gunned down at Rob Elementary School.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.170
You visited Uvalde as well.

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:02.003
- [Dr. Jill Biden] Yes.

00:01:02.003 --> 00:01:03.960
[Kelly] So, how did it
impact you as a teacher?

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.570
Cuz as a mother- a teacher
and a mother, but like,

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:09.060
how does it impact you
specifically, though, as a teacher?

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.490
- Oh my gosh. It was devastating.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:15.210
It was so devastating
when Joe and I, you know,

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:17.910
got out of the car and
there were those 19 crosses

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:20.160
for the children with their pictures.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.200
And Joe and I went up and put our hands

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:25.080
on each and every one, to know-

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:27.900
the parents know that
we saw their children.

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.960
And you know, having lost a child myself,

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.590
I mean it was, it was just
devastating when we met

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:37.920
with all those families
and the teachers' families.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:42.920
And so we know we've gotta
do more. We've gotta do more.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.773
We must ban assault weapons.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:53.390
- Shortly after the Uvalde shooting,

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:55.440
an Aurora, Colorado English teacher

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:59.220
posted this very powerful video on TikTok.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:00.780
- [Taylor Mora] The
reality of being a teacher

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:02.820
is making sure your door can quickly lock

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:04.710
in case there's an active threat.

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:08.130
It's having a window cover
you can quickly pull down.

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:10.170
Loving natural sunlight,
but having to pull

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:12.090
down your window cover so no one can see

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:13.620
inside your classroom.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:16.500
Making sure your safe place is
always clear and accessible,

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:18.060
in the case an active threat happens

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:19.743
during your conference or lunch.

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:22.800
- We have that teacher with us, right now.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:24.360
Please welcome Taylor Mora,

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:27.144
who teaches eighth grade
in Aurora, Colorado.

00:02:27.144 --> 00:02:30.510
(audience applauds)

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:33.240
- [Kelly] So, hello. Welcome to the show.

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:34.770
- [Taylor Mora] Thank
you. Thanks for having me.

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:37.070
- [Kelly] It was so powerful. Like-

00:02:37.070 --> 00:02:38.610
I just, I was telling you on the break,

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:41.400
I was like, "I remember
worrying about tornado drills

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.590
as a kid, not these kinds of things."

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:45.720
Like, is that why you made this video?

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:47.310
Cuz it's so insane?

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:49.560
Like what you're having to deal with?

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:51.870
- Yes. I made this video because I wanted

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:54.328
to show the daily reality that teachers,

00:02:54.328 --> 00:02:55.950
we have to go through and the things

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:58.440
that we have to think of.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:01.470
- I hate that I have to think
before leaving my husband

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:03.360
in the morning, you know, is this gonna be

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:04.740
the last time I'm gonna see you?

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:06.270
Is this gonna be the last
time I'm gonna kiss you?

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:09.093
But that's something
that I have to think of.

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:13.080
My previous school, we
had active shooter drills,

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:15.540
probably about three times a year.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:18.450
But that drill that I did in the video,

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:20.610
that's something I do every single day.

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:23.220
I don't allow my students
to see me do that

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:25.290
because I don't wanna terrify them.

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:27.780
I want them to be focused on learning.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:30.240
Even though doing that drill,
you know, it terrifies me

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:33.900
because what if, you know, that
day I have to implement it.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:38.900
I made that TikTok because I
want it to spark conversation,

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:41.400
and I want it to spark change.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:44.158
Because, absolutely, enough is enough.

00:03:44.158 --> 00:03:44.991
- Mhmm. Absolutely.

00:03:44.991 --> 00:03:47.991
(audience applauds)

00:03:48.987 --> 00:03:52.290
- [Kelly] How do we come together
and stop these tragedies?

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:53.850
I know you were talking
about assault weapons,

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:56.400
but do you also feel like, I mean,

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:58.980
every time I hear about the
tragedy of the lost students,

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:00.450
I always think about the shooter, too.

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:04.050
Cuz I'm like, what is happening
in that person's head?

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:06.060
Like, what is going on in their world

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:07.350
that that's where they went.

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:10.440
Like, we have also failed them. You know?

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.990
And it's- I mean, I don't know.

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:15.240
I don't know how this
gets fixed, you know?

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:17.130
- Well, you know, I think
there's several things, Kelly.

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:19.710
Like you said, I did this exact same thing

00:04:19.710 --> 00:04:21.690
in my classroom, last week.

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:25.470
I said to my students, "You
have to have a plan in your head

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:28.170
if there's an active shooter on campus."

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:30.960
And so, I did exactly what you do.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:35.070
But you know, we did give
more money for mental health,

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:38.940
so hopefully it'll help
kids in the schools

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:42.420
that are dealing with
problems, like the shooter.

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:45.930
And I think that we just
have to, to come together

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:50.930
and have common sense
gun laws, and pass them.

00:04:51.659 --> 00:04:53.130
(audience applauds)

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:56.493
- We are back with First
Lady, Dr. Jill Biden!

00:04:58.206 --> 00:05:01.230
So, let's talk to more of the
educators in our audience.

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:03.420
Tyson Jones is a teacher and principal

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:04.820
from my home state of Texas.

00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:07.450
What's your question, Mr. Jones?

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:12.180
- Hi, Kelly. Hi, Dr. Biden. First of all,

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:14.422
what an honor to be talking to you both.

00:05:14.422 --> 00:05:17.340
You could say some people
wait a lifetime for-

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:19.739
- [Kelly] Oh, you did it! Ah, you did it.

00:05:19.739 --> 00:05:22.290
(audience applauds and laughs)

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:24.510
- So, I've been in education for 14 years.

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:26.670
I taught second grade,
I taught fourth grade,

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:27.840
and now I'm the proud principal

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:30.030
at Sigler Elementary School in Plano,

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:31.680
home of the Sigler Stars. Woo-woo!

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:33.019
- [Kelly] Yeah!

00:05:33.019 --> 00:05:34.530
(audience applauds)

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:35.880
- [Tyson Jones] So our teachers-

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:38.550
I'm blessed. I have phenomenal
teachers at my campus.

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:40.969
And I've noticed though, just as a nation

00:05:40.969 --> 00:05:43.080
over the past few years, especially,

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:45.060
it seems like there's a growing amount

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:49.830
of distrust between schools
and parents and communities.

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:52.500
Whether that's how we are choosing

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:54.990
to solve a discipline situation,

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:58.080
or how we're addressing
Social Emotional Learning.

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:00.780
So, how would you suggest
that we, as educators,

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:04.590
can start to rebuild that trust
with us and our communities?

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:06.120
- [Dr. Biden] Well, you
know, during the pandemic,

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:10.417
I actually saw the reverse. I
saw where parents were saying,

00:06:10.417 --> 00:06:15.417
"Wow, this job is hard. Thank
goodness for what you do."

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:18.060
And I felt that parents
were really thankful

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:19.740
to all of the teachers.

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:23.280
But I think, you know, we
love, we love our students,

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:26.100
just like their parents
do. And I think maybe

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:29.490
it just comes down to
dialoguing with the parents.

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:31.530
And I don't know whether
other teachers here

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:34.892
have other suggestions, but-

00:06:34.892 --> 00:06:37.859
just this communication,
open communication

00:06:37.859 --> 00:06:41.070
with our student- to
bring everyone together,

00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:42.930
to be on the same page.

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:45.230
- I also think too, it's like-

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:48.540
it's like, one of my kids was held back.

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:49.950
And I think I get what
you're saying, too, though,

00:06:49.950 --> 00:06:52.350
because they - the teachers
and the principal -

00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:54.030
they were afraid, almost, to tell me

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:55.319
and my husband at the time

00:06:55.319 --> 00:06:57.690
that we were gonna have to
hold back one of our kids.

00:06:57.690 --> 00:06:59.719
And we were like, "Okay."

00:06:59.719 --> 00:07:02.098
But they were like, "Oh my
God, that never happens.

00:07:02.098 --> 00:07:04.890
Usually, parents get
so frustrated that we-"

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:06.768
And we were like, well
no, we recognize that,

00:07:06.768 --> 00:07:08.970
you know, she kind of
is having a hard time

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:09.930
and we can't figure out what it is.

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:11.340
She's too young to test. Like we can't-

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:12.336
We're like, "Totally cool."

00:07:12.336 --> 00:07:13.169
- Yeah.

00:07:13.169 --> 00:07:15.720
- But most of the time, it was
like they've been battered.

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:17.490
Like, they were- like,
literally they were just like

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:19.260
they get beat up anytime
they come to parents.

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:20.550
And parents, you gotta know, like,

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:23.010
all kids learn at different
times. Like, you know,

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:24.180
they grow differently.
- [Dr. Biden] We know that.

00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:25.013
Right?
- Yeah.

00:07:25.013 --> 00:07:27.390
But I think the thing is that
parents should know that too.

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:29.460
Parents, I think you expect
everything, and it's like,

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:32.220
just how I grew up and I
didn't have to repeat a grade,

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:33.990
but so what? One of my
kids does, and that's-

00:07:33.990 --> 00:07:36.660
and now she's better,
more prepared, you know?

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:38.160
Read "The Tipping Point."

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:38.993
Anyway.
(Dr. Biden laughs)

00:07:38.993 --> 00:07:41.760
So, thank you so much,
Mr. Jones, for joining us.

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:42.909
- [Dr. Biden] Thank you.

00:07:42.909 --> 00:07:43.990
(audience applauds)

00:07:43.990 --> 00:07:45.660
- [Kelly] Next, I agree, though, I guess.

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:47.250
Communication between
teachers and parents-

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:49.740
it's so important that you
have that relationship.

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:52.440
Next is Ericca Dent,
a second grade teacher

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:55.433
at Joaquin Miller Elementary
in Burbank, California.

00:07:55.433 --> 00:07:58.140
Hello. How are you doing today?

00:07:58.140 --> 00:07:59.586
- Good, thank you. Thank you.

00:07:59.586 --> 00:08:01.740
- [Kelly] So, what's your question?

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:03.390
- Well, hi Kelly. Hi, Dr. Biden.

00:08:03.390 --> 00:08:04.230
- Hello.

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:05.910
- [Ericca Dent] I
absolutely love teaching.

00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:09.150
I knew from a very young
age that I wanted to teach,

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:10.650
when I thought passing out papers was

00:08:10.650 --> 00:08:13.650
the coolest thing you could
do. And then, as I grew older,

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:16.890
I knew I wanted to help shape lives.

00:08:16.890 --> 00:08:19.140
And it wasn't until my ninth grade year

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:21.360
that I had my first teacher of Color.

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:24.420
So, I know the importance
of representation.

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:26.940
But I am concerned. I know that I feel,

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:29.760
and I'm pretty sure other
educators in the room feel,

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:31.440
that teaching is getting harder.

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:34.290
I know not many people are
entering the profession

00:08:34.290 --> 00:08:36.720
and so many teachers are
considering quitting.

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:39.210
What do you think we should do to be able

00:08:39.210 --> 00:08:41.730
to keep this diverse group of teachers,

00:08:41.730 --> 00:08:44.130
and then also help feel supported?

00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:45.960
- [Dr. Biden] So I think, you know,

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:49.740
we are our own best advocates,
I think, for the profession.

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:54.740
We know how much we love
teaching. It's who we are, right?

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:57.420
And so, I think we are the ones.

00:08:57.420 --> 00:08:59.520
We have to be the messengers that go out

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:02.010
and spread the word. Like,
if you're in some job

00:09:02.010 --> 00:09:04.620
that you don't like, come and teach.

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:06.720
Because you'll find meaning every day.

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:09.390
You'll find that you can
change someone's life.

00:09:09.390 --> 00:09:12.180
And what our administration
is doing, which I love,

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:16.140
is that they are now
paying for apprenticeships.

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:17.242
We're gonna pay you

00:09:17.242 --> 00:09:20.670
until you get your teaching certification.

00:09:20.670 --> 00:09:22.620
So now these teaching apprenticeships

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:24.540
are one of the things
that we're aiming for.

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:28.233
Plus, we must have more teachers of Color.

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:31.860
- [Kelly] And I know that you
said it was important for you

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:33.240
to see someone that maybe looked like you,

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:35.310
when you were a kid, but it's
also important for my kid

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:36.720
to see all different walks of life.

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:37.553
- [Dr. Biden] Yeah.

00:09:37.553 --> 00:09:39.000
- [Kelly] Like that's, I think
that's just as important.

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:42.450
Like, representation isn't
just for one type of person.

00:09:42.450 --> 00:09:44.521
It's for everyone to see everything.

00:09:44.521 --> 00:09:46.146
(audience applauds)
That's the important part.

00:09:46.146 --> 00:09:48.330
- [Dr. Biden] Yeah.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:50.163
Thank you so much, Ms. Dent!

